Subject: [PATCH] router: clean up with the client that created the router

Partial backport of upstream neutron-tempest-plugin 5431c1424 ("Fix admin
router cleanup and remove BaseRouterTest class",
Change-Id: I6bd79e7bfbc5e496009fed53dfe8d20f5f3af748).

BaseRouterTest._create_router() accepts a client, creates the router with it,
and then registers cleanup without it:

    client = client or self.client
    router = self._create_router_with_client(client, ...)
    self.addCleanup(self._cleanup_router, router)

_cleanup_router(router, client=None) forwards to delete_router(router, client),
which falls back to cls.client -- the primary project's client. When the router
was created with a different project's client, that fallback cannot see it:

    def _cleanup_router(self, router, client=None):
        try:
            self.delete_router(router, client)
            self.routers.remove(router)
        except exceptions.NotFound:
            pass

delete_router() starts by listing the router's interfaces, which returns 404
RouterNotFound for a router the primary project cannot see. _cleanup_router
catches NotFound and passes, so cleanup reports success without having issued
a single DELETE. Class cleanup retries with the same wrong client and swallows
the same 404. Credential cleanup then deletes the owning project, and the
router outlives both the test and its project.

Two tests in api/test_routers.py:ExternalGWMultihomingRoutersTest pass
client=self.admin_client and attach an external gateway:

    test_create_router_add_external_gateways_compat
    test_create_router_remove_external_gateways_compat

Both report ok while leaving a router behind that holds an address out of the
floating-IP range for the life of the cloud. Because the owning project is
gone, neither a project-scoped purge nor a network-scoped teardown reaches it,
and nothing but the creation timestamp links it back to the run. On a
long-lived cloud this accumulates until the external range is exhausted --
observed at roughly two routers per full-suite run, across releases and even
on fully green runs.

Pass the creation client to cleanup. This is the same client the router was
created with, already defaulted to self.client on the line above, so the
non-admin path is unchanged. It also makes _create_router consistent with
_create_admin_router directly below it, which already hands its client to
_cleanup_router.

Verified A/B against a live 2026.1 cluster, running only
ExternalGWMultihomingRoutersTest (10 tests, all passing) on the stock image
and then on the same image plus this patch: 2 routers leaked before, 0 after.

Why this is carried here rather than fixed by a version bump: upstream merged
5431c1424 on 2026-08-25, after neutron-tempest-plugin 3.2.0 was tagged
(2026-05-04), and it is in no tagged release -- 3.2.0 is still the newest. The
upstream change goes further, removing BaseRouterTest entirely and moving
router cleanup into BaseNetworkTest.resource_cleanup across eleven files; only
the leak-fixing line is taken here. Drop this patch as soon as the installed
neutron-tempest-plugin contains 5431c1424, at which point base_routers.py is
gone and the Containerfile's dry-run will fail the build and force exactly
that.

--- a/neutron_tempest_plugin/api/base_routers.py
+++ b/neutron_tempest_plugin/api/base_routers.py
@@ -39,7 +39,7 @@
             client, router_name=name, admin_state_up=admin_state_up,
             external_network_id=external_network_id, enable_snat=enable_snat,
             **kwargs)
-        self.addCleanup(self._cleanup_router, router)
+        self.addCleanup(self._cleanup_router, router, client)
         return router
 
     def _create_admin_router(self, *args, **kwargs):
